WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.720
When do closure proofs need

2
00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:04.080
deciders or dovetailing? A decider

3
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.020
always halts, while a recognizer

4
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.480
may loop on inputs outside

5
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.040
its language. We will compare

6
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.060
algorithms for union, complement and

7
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.140
factorization so that the machine-class

8
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.000
hypothesis stays visible in every

9
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:15.220
proof.

10
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.960
For the union of two

11
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.280
decidable languages, run the first

12
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.060
decider and accept if it

13
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.300
accepts, otherwise run the second.

14
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.020
Each call halts, so the

15
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.960
sequence always reaches a final

16
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.400
verdict. The same sequential idea

17
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.580
works for intersection by requiring

18
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.740
acceptance from both.

19
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.680
Now suppose the machines are

20
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.900
only recognizers. A word could

21
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.220
belong to the second language

22
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.900
while the first recognizer loops

23
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.640
on it forever, so a

24
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.540
serial union algorithm never reaches

25
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.760
the accepting second test. Alternate

26
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.080
one step of each simulation

27
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.780
instead, accepting when either accepts.

28
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.060
For recognizable intersection, accept only

29
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.200
after both simulations have accepted.

30
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.300
Alternating them is a uniform

31
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.860
safe construction, and serial execution

32
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.540
also works for this particular

33
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.900
membership condition because both accept

34
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.660
on every true member. On

35
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.700
nonmembers, rejection or nonhalting is

36
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.860
permitted, but false acceptance is

37
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:12.100
not.

38
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.880
Complementing a decider swaps its

39
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.560
accept and reject outcomes. Because

40
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.820
it always halts, this inverts

41
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.340
the verdict on every input.

42
00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.060
Swapping a recognizer’s halting outcomes

43
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.200
leaves its loops unchanged, so

44
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.300
a nonmember on which it

45
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.760
loops is still not accepted

46
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.840
by the supposed complement recognizer.

47
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.860
For concatenation of decidable languages,

48
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.920
test all input splits, including

49
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.760
an empty prefix or suffix.

50
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.800
A length-n word has n

51
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.860
plus one split positions, and

52
00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.300
every pair of decider runs

53
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.860
finishes. Accept if one split

54
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.320
passes both tests, otherwise reject

55
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.940
after the finite list is

56
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:51.380
exhausted.

57
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.120
For star, accept epsilon immediately,

58
00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.200
then consider factorizations into nonempty

59
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.020
pieces. There are finitely many

60
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.660
ways to place cuts between

61
00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:03.840
the symbols of a fixed

62
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.960
word, and every decider test

63
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.720
terminates. This proves decidability even

64
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.360
though trying all cuts is

65
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.460
not the efficient P-class construction

66
00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:14.420
we study later.

67
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.460
For recognizers, testing those candidate

68
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.100
factorizations serially can hang on

69
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.300
the wrong one. Dovetail the

70
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.560
finitely many candidate piece-computations and

71
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.420
accept once every piece of

72
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.140
some candidate has accepted. Equivalently,

73
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.280
guess a factorization nondeterministically and

74
00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.340
use NTM-DTM recognition equivalence.

75
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.020
The PATH example illustrates a

76
00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.460
positive halting argument. Mark the

77
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.660
source vertex, propagate marks along

78
00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.120
edges until a full pass

79
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.400
adds none, then test whether

80
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.120
the target is marked. Marks

81
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.360
are never removed and only

82
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.220
finitely many vertices can be

83
00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.020
newly marked, so the procedure

84
00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.640
halts on every valid finite

85
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:56.020
graph.

86
00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.500
The Church Turing thesis connects

87
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.480
effectively executable algorithms with Turing

88
00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.420
computation, but it is a

89
00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.760
thesis rather than a theorem

90
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.400
equating two fully formal definitions.

91
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.960
Distinguish formal transitions, implementation-level scanning

92
00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.140
descriptions and high-level algorithms. A

93
00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.920
high-level input such as a

94
00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.340
graph still arrives as an

95
00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.640
encoded finite string, and malformed

96
00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.640
encodings need a rejection rule.

97
00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.300
Things to take note of.

98
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.280
Separate correct membership behaviour from

99
00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.360
the additional requirement to halt,

100
00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.420
and never schedule a potentially

101
00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.580
endless recognizer before a result

102
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.320
you still need. Use finite

103
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.500
factor lists or fair interleaving

104
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.480
with the appropriate acceptance condition,

105
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.520
and do not infer efficient

106
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.820
runtime merely from decidability.
